  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">New Approach to Building a Participatory and Inclusive Marketplace for Sustainable Energy Solutions</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Limited access to cooking fuel, lighting, electricity, and other energy services in displacement settings have exposed refugees and internally displaced persons to increased hardships and gender-based violence risks while putting pressure on surrounding host communities and threatening the balance of environmental ecosystems. Even though providing sustainable energy access is starting to be seen as a priority in the humanitarian sector, very little data is available on the current level of access. The best estimation dates from 2015, back when the Moving Energy Initiative estimated that 80% of displaced people in camps have absolutely minimal access to energy, with high dependence on traditional biomass for cooking and no access to electricity. Moreover, basic energy products are often acquired through non-food item distribution preventing displaced people from having flexibility and dignity in their choice, and potentially disrupting the local economy. This project aims to bridge the gap between the demand for sustainable energy solutions and the supply of products/services from the private sector, by overcoming the economic barriers for displaced population and host communities to access them and reducing the risks for private actors to enter the market in displacement settings.</narrative>
  </description>
